  "body": "## Why\n\nThe plan usage popover showed Claude Code as \"read 20h ago\", with 100% of its 5-hour window left after a day of Claude agents running, and no new reading came for an hour after Althar restarted. The coordinator steers work by the same numbers, and `list_models` passed them on.\n\nThe logs on the machine where this was seen show three things:\n\n- Most of the 20 hours, Althar wasn't running. It was closed from 23:33 to 19:23 UTC the next day, and its last reading was from 23:29.\n- After the restart, the next reading from Anthropic's `/api/oauth/usage` only came an hour later. Why isn't established. The log didn't record the status, and the popover's \"The provider asked Althar to wait\" message appeared after any failed read, not only a 429. During that hour, the same request sent by hand got a 200 with both user agents, so the default version in the user agent isn't the cause.\n- The real reason the numbers went stale is that every reading from Claude's own sessions was dropped. With the current Claude Code CLI, a `rate_limit_event` carries `utilization` for its own window only near the limit. Every window the CLI tracks is in its `unifiedWindows`. A captured event from a real session read `rateLimitType: \"five_hour\"` with no `utilization`, and `unifiedWindows` with five_hour 0.3 and seven_day 0.62. Althar read only the first, so with agents running all day it kept nothing and fell back on the endpoint.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Session events first.** Every window in a session's `rate_limit_event` is kept, from `unifiedWindows`, falling back to the event's own window where that's all there is.\n- **The endpoint as a fallback.** It's asked only for an account no session has reported on for 15 minutes, still at most every 5 minutes, and still waiting as long as a 429 asks. Each 429 is now logged with its Retry-After.\n- **Session readings win.** An endpoint answer is dated when it was asked, not when it arrived. It therefore never replaces what a session said while it was in flight, including when the reader gives it again from memory during a backoff, or through a request shared by two callers. A refusal to a question asked before a session reported is dropped too.\n- **What the reader says.** Inside the interval after a read that got no windows, it repeats what that read said (an error, an expired sign-in, no sign-in), instead of claiming the provider asked for a wait.\n- **Reset windows are unknown.** A window whose reset has passed is left out of the runtime's view, as steering already left it out. The gauge, the peek and the model pickers also leave it out by their own clock, so one that resets while the peek is open drops out at once rather than showing all of its plan left.\n\n## Approach\n\n`unifiedWindows` is internal to the CLI and could change. Where it's missing, Althar reads the event's own window as before and falls back to the endpoint, so the worst case is the old behaviour. Dating endpoint answers when they were asked is conservative: the server's figures could be from any time up to the response. That only matters against a session reading made during the request, and there the session's should win.\n\n## How it was checked\n\nThis branch's build was run with a real Claude Code coordinator and lead, using a real sign-in. The plan peek showed a fresh reading with no age and no wait message, and the stored reading's source was the session events, not the endpoint. The real-agent spec now asserts both. The endpoint replay after a 429 can't be arranged by hand, so it's covered by tests only.\n\nOpened by [Althar](https://github.com/AltharIDE/althar).",
